1
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:11,310
LEGO began in 1932 as a carpenter's workshop in Billund, Denmark, making wooden toys during the Depression.

2
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:15,330
The name came from two Danish words: "leg godt."

3
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:16,450
Play well.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,610
In 1947, the carpenter, Ole Kirk Christiansen, bought a plastic injection-molding machine.

5
00:00:23,660 --> 00:00:28,350
It reportedly cost more than twice what the company had earned the year before.

6
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:31,710
By 1949, it was making little plastic bricks.

7
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:40,570
According to LEGO's own history, in 1954 his son Godtfred heard a toy buyer complain that toys had no system.

8
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:43,170
Nothing connected to anything else.

9
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:45,230
So Godtfred built one.

10
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:51,830
A town to build, where every piece fits every other piece, and every new set adds to the ones you already have.

11
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:53,870
LEGO launched it the next year.

12
00:00:54,620 --> 00:00:56,270
There was just one problem.

13
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:58,450
The bricks didn't hold together very well.

14
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:01,390
So Godtfred added tubes under each brick.

15
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:04,410
In 1958, LEGO filed the patent.

16
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:05,890
Now every brick locked.

17
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:12,010
Two years later, a fire destroyed the company's warehouse of wooden toys.

18
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:13,870
LEGO didn't rebuild that part.

19
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:15,430
It focused on the bricks.

20
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:18,550
Keep those two ideas in mind.

21
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:22,550
A system where the same pieces keep coming back, set after set.

22
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:25,830
And a company that grew stronger by doing less.

23
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:28,530
Four decades later, LEGO would forget both.
